test_tcp_fr — TCP fast retransmit (RFC 5681 §3.2)
====================================================

Connects to the host echo server on 10.0.2.2:18080, arms the
nic loss injector to drop the first TX after connect, then
pipelines 1920 bytes (four MSS-sized segments). The first
segment is dropped; the host's ACKs for segments 2, 3, 4 all
re-ACK the byte we're still waiting on, producing three pure
duplicate ACKs. Slix's ACK handler should count them and fire
`inet_tcp_fast_retransmit` well before the RTO fires.

Observable via `tcp_debug`:

- Before fast retransmit: `snd_ssthresh == 65535` (initial,
  never halved).
- After: `snd_ssthresh < 65535` — only the fast-retransmit or
  RTO collapse paths can lower it, and the test is scheduled
  so RTO can't have fired in time.

Harness asserts the ssthresh transition and that the payload
eventually bounces back through the echo (proving the
retransmit actually got the lost segment re-delivered).

Output:

```
test_tcp_fr: connected fd=N
test_tcp_fr: initial ssthresh=65535 cwnd=1440
test_tcp_fr: arm drop_next=1
test_tcp_fr: sent=1920
test_tcp_fr: after ssthresh=N cwnd=M
test_tcp_fr: ok
```

    module oskit.bin.test_tcp_fr

    import oskit.ulib.{puts, exit, exit_with, sleep}
    import oskit.net.{tcp_connect, tcp_send, tcp_close, tcp_debug,
        TcpDebug, nic_debug_drop_next}
    import std.net.ipv4

    print_udec(v: int)
        if v >= 10
            print_udec(v / 10)
        var buf: [1]byte
        buf[0] = byte('0' + (v % 10))
        puts(string(&buf[0], 1))

    print_int(prefix: string, v: int)
        puts(prefix)
        if v < 0
            puts("-")
            print_udec(0 - v)
        else
            print_udec(v)


    main(argv: []string)
        val dst = ipv4(10, 0, 2, 2)
        var fd = -1
        var attempts = 0
        while fd < 0 && attempts < 5
            fd = tcp_connect(dst, 18080)
            if fd < 0
                sleep(20)
            attempts += 1
        if fd < 0
            puts("test_tcp_fr: connect failed\n")
            exit_with(1)
            return
        print_int("test_tcp_fr: connected fd=", fd)
        puts("\n")

        var d0: TcpDebug
        if tcp_debug(fd, &d0) < 0
            puts("test_tcp_fr: debug(initial) failed\n")
            tcp_close(fd)
            exit_with(2)
            return
        puts("test_tcp_fr: initial")
        print_int(" ssthresh=", d0.snd_ssthresh)
        print_int(" cwnd=", d0.snd_cwnd)
        puts("\n")

Arm the one-shot loss injector. The very next TCP data segment
we emit will be dropped by the nic before it hits the wire.

        if nic_debug_drop_next(1) < 0
            puts("test_tcp_fr: arm drop failed\n")
            tcp_close(fd)
            exit_with(3)
            return
        puts("test_tcp_fr: arm drop_next=1\n")

        var payload: [1920]byte
        var j = 0
        while j < 1920
            payload[j] = byte(j & 0xff)
            j += 1
        // tcp_send() caps at INET_MAX_TCP_SEG (480) per call, so loop
        // to push all four segments. The second, third, fourth go
        // through normally — only the first gets dropped by the nic.
        // Those three delivered segments trigger three dup-ACKs on
        // the host side, which is exactly what fast retransmit
        // needs to fire.
        var total = 0
        while total < 1920
            val chunk = 480
            var want = chunk
            if total + want > 1920
                want = 1920 - total
            val sent = tcp_send(fd, &payload[total], want)
            if sent < 0
                puts("test_tcp_fr: send failed\n")
                tcp_close(fd)
                exit_with(4)
                return
            if sent == 0
                sleep(2)
            else
                total += sent
        print_int("test_tcp_fr: sent=", total)
        puts("\n")

Wait long enough for host ACKs to arrive — the three duplicate
ACKs arrive within one RTT (~tens of ms on slirp). Our RTO is
~1 s initially, so 100 ms is well inside the pre-RTO window.

        sleep(10)

        var d1: TcpDebug
        if tcp_debug(fd, &d1) < 0
            puts("test_tcp_fr: debug(after) failed\n")
            tcp_close(fd)
            exit_with(5)
            return
        puts("test_tcp_fr: after")
        print_int(" ssthresh=", d1.snd_ssthresh)
        print_int(" cwnd=", d1.snd_cwnd)
        puts("\n")

Phase-3 observables: recovery exited cleanly, no data still
unacknowledged. If either is wrong the harness catches it
without having to reason about timing-sensitive cwnd inflate
amounts.

        print_int("test_tcp_fr: fr=", d1.fast_recovery)
        puts("\n")
        if d1.snd_una == d1.snd_nxt
            puts("test_tcp_fr: una==nxt\n")
        else
            puts("test_tcp_fr: una!=nxt")
            print_int(" una=", d1.snd_una)
            print_int(" nxt=", d1.snd_nxt)
            puts("\n")

Disable drop injection so nothing on the way out is affected.

        nic_debug_drop_next(0)
        tcp_close(fd)
        puts("test_tcp_fr: ok\n")
        exit()
